I've read a _lot_ of both Fantasy and fairly Hard SF about the
disbenefits of living "forever", and it was my conceit that the
Mage was sort of the last stage of an elven life when all else
bored one to tears, before choosing "to turn into mist on the
mountain" (or "take the boat to the Eternal Fields", again
depending on "YMOC"), and, without having deeply considered it,
that the enfeeblement might be a rite of passage or irreversable
tradeoff as a requirement of the craft of some such.  Ever read
Niven's _World of Ptavvs_, if I nailed that spelling, where
there is a similar end stage of a potentially immortal life?

> And what is "most of" an immortal life, anyway?

The part just before the part where you realize that eventually
all that will be left that attracts you is _choosing_ death.

After all, in a race where any significant number actually
_achieved_ immortality, children would be pointless.  Think of
all the elves you saw die in The Two Towers, fighting for
something they finally realized was more important than life
itself even for immortals, the right to be unashamed, and also
of all the parental relationships portrayed.  Immortality,
logically, is _always_ only "potential immortality", after all,
unless you have the enormous misfortune to be some luckless
ill-conceived deity with no way to dump the workload, and even
Atlas managed to score a rest once.

xanthian, resigned, calm, atheist "vaguely senior citizen",
